  "rationale": "Buruk and teruk are valid preferred forms for the intended sense, but the national split is materially overstated: buruk is also an accepted Malay equivalent, while teruk is also an Indonesian dictionary entry, chiefly for severe or very bad conditions. The relation should not be different-common-word; it should indicate shared semantic overlap with regional preference. The English cue is sound, and no false-friend contrast is warranted.",
  "claim_reviews": [
    {
      "subject": "English cue and sense",
      "conclusion": "The cue 'very bad or unpleasant' accurately represents the relevant sense of terrible.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "[Cambridge Dictionary: terrible](https://dictionary.cambridge.org/dictionary/english/terrible)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Indonesian answer buruk",
      "conclusion": "Buruk is a valid Indonesian answer for bad, unpleasant, or otherwise negative qualities.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "[KBBI entry: buruk](https://kbbi.web.id/buruk)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Malaysian Malay answer teruk",
      "conclusion": "Teruk is a valid Malaysian Malay answer, including the sense of something being extremely bad, though it also strongly covers severity, seriousness, or difficulty.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "[PRPM/Kamus Dewan entry: teruk](https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=254640&keyword=teruk)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Cross-variety overlap and final relation",
      "conclusion": "The relation different-common-word is materially misleading. Malay buruk means not good or unpleasant, and Indonesian teruk is an accepted dictionary word meaning severe, hard, or very bad; therefore the forms are not cleanly variety-exclusive. They are better characterized as overlapping equivalents with regional preference and sense-frequency differences.",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "[PRPM/Kamus Dewan entry: buruk](https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1.aspx?d=299476&keyword=buruk)",
        "[KBBI entry: teruk](https://kbbi.web.id/teruk)",
        "[PRPM/Kamus Dewan entry: teruk](https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=254640&keyword=teruk)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "False-friend contrast",
      "conclusion": "No false-friend contrast is needed. The forms differ in preference and collocational emphasis, not through a deceptive opposing meaning; the empty contrast field is acceptable.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_limitation": "Dictionary evidence establishes overlapping meanings but does not by itself quantify everyday national frequency or preference."
    }
  ]
